WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.173 --> 00:00:04.580
David Barron: Hey Ben, can you hear me?

2
00:00:05.381 --> 00:00:10.443
Ben Adeyemi: Yep, loud and clear. Sorry, I'm two minutes late, the seminar ran over.

3
00:00:11.178 --> 00:00:16.393
David Barron: No worries at all. Before we start, is it okay if I record?

4
00:00:16.822 --> 00:00:22.330
David Barron: It's just for my notes, I won't share it outside me and my co-founder.

5
00:00:23.068 --> 00:00:24.557
Ben Adeyemi: Yeah, that's fine.

6
00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:33.876
David Barron: Great, thanks. So, um, quick context. I'm on leave from a PhD at Virginia Tech, and I'm building a research notebook with a friend.

7
00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:38.558
David Barron: The idea is a notebook that remembers where your thoughts came from.

8
00:00:38.814 --> 00:00:47.620
David Barron: So these chats are me trying to understand how people actually work, not a pitch. I'm not going to try to sell you anything.

9
00:00:48.112 --> 00:00:49.292
Ben Adeyemi: Ha, okay. Good.

10
00:00:50.381 --> 00:00:55.934
David Barron: So maybe start with what you're working on right now, at a high level.

11
00:00:56.694 --> 00:01:01.878
Ben Adeyemi: Sure. I'm a postdoc in stats. Mostly Bayesian hierarchical models for, um, small-area estimation.

12
00:01:02.288 --> 00:01:11.366
Ben Adeyemi: Right now it's county-level estimates of health insurance coverage, where some counties have like twelve survey respondents and you have to borrow strength from neighbors.

13
00:01:20.219 --> 00:01:26.444
David Barron: Okay. And what does a working day look like? Like, where does the work physically live?

14
00:01:27.799 --> 00:01:36.253
Ben Adeyemi: Physically, it's a Quarto project. R, Stan, a bunch of Quarto documents. Everything's in a git repo. Then there's Zotero for papers.

15
00:01:36.663 --> 00:01:45.847
Ben Adeyemi: And then there's a file called notes dot md at the root of every project, which is the honest record of what I was thinking.

16
00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:49.731
David Barron: Oh, interesting. Tell me about notes dot md.

17
00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:57.386
Ben Adeyemi: So it's just a dated log. Every day I work on the project I write a line or two.

18
00:01:57.545 --> 00:02:04.485
Ben Adeyemi: "Tried the BYM2 prior, divergences in the north region, suspect the scaling." Stuff like that. It's terrible prose.

19
00:02:04.692 --> 00:02:11.906
Ben Adeyemi: But six months later, it's the only thing that tells me why the model looks the way it does.

20
00:02:13.351 --> 00:02:16.742
David Barron: How often do you actually go back to it?

21
00:02:17.636 --> 00:02:23.563
Ben Adeyemi: Mostly when a reviewer asks "why did you choose this prior". Which is, like, every paper.

22
00:02:24.035 --> 00:02:31.220
Ben Adeyemi: And then I'm grepping notes dot md and git log at the same time trying to reconstruct the decision.

23
00:02:32.872 --> 00:02:36.936
David Barron: Can you walk me through the last time that happened?

24
00:02:38.305 --> 00:02:45.739
Ben Adeyemi: Yeah, um, a few months ago. Reviewer asked why we used a particular spatial structure instead of the simpler one.

25
00:02:46.174 --> 00:02:55.198
Ben Adeyemi: And I knew there was a reason. I found the git commit where I switched, the message just said "switch to BYM2, see notes".

26
00:02:55.587 --> 00:03:02.349
Ben Adeyemi: And notes dot md for that week said "per conversation with Lauren". And I don't remember the conversation.

27
00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:04.609
David Barron: Oh no.

28
00:03:05.703 --> 00:03:06.803
Ben Adeyemi: Right.

29
00:03:07.248 --> 00:03:16.866
Ben Adeyemi: So then I'm searching my Slack DMs with my advisor, my email, and eventually I find it in a Slack thread where she linked a paper.

30
00:03:17.017 --> 00:03:21.888
Ben Adeyemi: That paper was the reason. Took me, I don't know, forty minutes.

31
00:03:22.833 --> 00:03:27.903
David Barron: So the chain was commit, notes file, a person's name, Slack, a paper.

32
00:03:28.310 --> 00:03:32.387
Ben Adeyemi: Exactly. Five hops. And each hop is a different app.

33
00:03:34.046 --> 00:03:40.936
David Barron: That's, um, honestly that's almost exactly the thing we keep hearing. Do you use any AI tools in this?

34
00:03:45.343 --> 00:03:53.797
Ben Adeyemi: Some. I use ChatGPT for Stan syntax and for debugging divergences, and sometimes Claude for writing. But those chats are a black hole.

35
00:03:54.026 --> 00:04:01.670
Ben Adeyemi: I'll have a really good explanation of some reparameterization trick in a chat and then I can never find it again.

36
00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:07.966
David Barron: Do you ever copy things out of the chats into notes dot md?

37
00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:20.287
Ben Adeyemi: Sometimes. If it's important I'll paste a snippet with "from ChatGPT" in front of it. But I'm not consistent.

38
00:04:20.615 --> 00:04:26.740
Ben Adeyemi: And I'm a bit wary of it, because half the time the explanation is subtly wrong.

39
00:04:36.564 --> 00:04:41.403
David Barron: Yeah. Okay. What about papers? How do papers connect to the work?

40
00:04:42.272 --> 00:04:46.682
Ben Adeyemi: Zotero, with a collection per project. I highlight in the PDF reader.

41
00:04:46.926 --> 00:04:52.600
Ben Adeyemi: But the link from a highlight to a modeling decision is only in my head.

42
00:04:52.848 --> 00:04:57.500
Ben Adeyemi: Or in notes dot md if I was being good that day.

43
00:05:04.637 --> 00:05:11.212
David Barron: If you could ask your past self one question at any moment, what would it usually be?

44
00:05:12.247 --> 00:05:21.372
Ben Adeyemi: "Why." Why this prior, why this exclusion, why did I drop those three counties. The what is in the code. The why is scattered.

45
00:05:22.770 --> 00:05:26.019
David Barron: That's a good line. Can I steal that?

46
00:05:26.978 --> 00:05:28.200
Ben Adeyemi: Ha, sure.

47
00:05:29.205 --> 00:05:35.579
David Barron: Let me ask about the opposite direction. Is there anything you'd be uncomfortable with a tool reading?

48
00:05:35.682 --> 00:05:39.593
David Barron: Like if a notebook connected to your email and Slack.

49
00:05:40.608 --> 00:05:49.414
Ben Adeyemi: Email, yes. I'd be uncomfortable. There's reviewing stuff in there, confidential things, recommendation letters. Slack with my advisor, maybe, if I could choose channels.

50
00:05:49.469 --> 00:05:52.785
Ben Adeyemi: Git and Zotero and my notes, totally fine.

51
00:05:54.138 --> 00:05:56.460
David Barron: So per-source and per-channel control.

52
00:05:57.414 --> 00:06:06.745
Ben Adeyemi: Per channel at least. And I'd want to see what it pulled in before it does anything with it. Like a list I can uncheck.

53
00:06:07.995 --> 00:06:16.312
David Barron: That's really useful. We have a, um, a connect screen right now and it's kind of all-or-nothing per app. So that's good feedback.

54
00:06:17.325 --> 00:06:21.364
Ben Adeyemi: Yeah, all-or-nothing on email would be a no for me.

55
00:06:22.266 --> 00:06:27.983
David Barron: Okay. Last couple of questions. Do you ever work with collaborators in the same notes?

56
00:06:29.251 --> 00:06:36.971
Ben Adeyemi: Not in notes dot md, that's mine. With collaborators it's a Google Doc for the paper and an Overleaf later.

57
00:06:37.067 --> 00:06:44.707
Ben Adeyemi: And a shared Quarto site for results, which is actually nice because each figure has the code next to it.

58
00:06:46.296 --> 00:06:49.973
David Barron: Is that site something you'd point a tool at?

59
00:06:51.572 --> 00:06:55.337
Ben Adeyemi: Probably, yeah. It's the most cleaned-up version of the reasoning.

60
00:06:56.985 --> 00:07:04.357
David Barron: Okay, cool. And, um, if something like this existed, what would make you actually open it? Like, what's the moment?

61
00:07:05.198 --> 00:07:06.600
Ben Adeyemi: Reviewer responses. Honestly.

62
00:07:06.924 --> 00:07:17.441
Ben Adeyemi: If I could paste a reviewer question and it showed me the commit, the note, the Slack thread and the paper, in order, I'd use it every revision.

63
00:07:17.847 --> 00:07:19.067
Ben Adeyemi: That's the moment.

64
00:07:19.597 --> 00:07:27.780
David Barron: That's super concrete. Thank you. Would you be up for trying an early version, maybe in a month or so? No pressure.

65
00:07:29.085 --> 00:07:33.255
Ben Adeyemi: Sure, as long as I can keep email out of it.

66
00:07:34.033 --> 00:07:35.490
David Barron: Deal. Email out.

67
00:07:35.821 --> 00:07:46.099
David Barron: I'll send you a short note after this with what we talked about, and a link to the sign-up sheet if you want to be on the list.

68
00:07:47.512 --> 00:07:50.577
Ben Adeyemi: Sounds good. Nice to meet you, David.

69
00:07:51.311 --> 00:07:55.015
David Barron: You too, thanks so much Ben. Stopping the recording now.
